WEBVTT

00:00:01.150 --> 00:00:03.790
You want a league table for eight schools.

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:08.210
The results are out there, spread across eight websites, on pages

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:09.870
made for people to read.

00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:15.850
A scraper is a program that downloads those pages and pulls rows

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:18.950
out of them. The simplest one is a single loop.

00:00:20.598 --> 00:00:24.978
For each site: download the page, find the results table, read each

00:00:24.998 --> 00:00:27.438
row, and add it to the standings.

00:00:27.878 --> 00:00:30.078
On a good day, it works.

00:00:32.078 --> 00:00:33.718
Then one site doesn't answer.

00:00:33.918 --> 00:00:38.218
The loop waits, gives up, and the whole run fails, taking the finished

00:00:38.238 --> 00:00:39.398
sites down with it.

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:43.850
So downloading becomes its own part:

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:48.830
scrape. It works from a list of addresses, checks the answer each

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:52.010
site sends back, waits, and tries again.

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:56.430
When a site stays silent, scrape has to do something about it, and

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:59.130
what it should do is a real decision.

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:00.650
Hold on to that.

00:01:03.034 --> 00:01:04.834
A month later you find a bug.

00:01:04.954 --> 00:01:07.714
The parser swapped home and away scores.

00:01:07.994 --> 00:01:11.814
You fix it and go back for the pages, but the sites have moved on,

00:01:11.834 --> 00:01:14.434
and some of last month's pages are gone.

00:01:16.034 --> 00:01:17.674
So the next part is store.

00:01:17.874 --> 00:01:22.694
Save every page exactly as it arrived, before any of your code reads

00:01:22.714 --> 00:01:25.194
it. Now a bug is cheap.

00:01:25.314 --> 00:01:29.214
Fix the parser and run it again on the saved pages, without asking

00:01:29.234 --> 00:01:30.474
the web for anything.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:34.790
The third break is quieter.

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:38.730
A site is redesigned, and the results are no longer the first table

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:42.790
on the page. The loop reads the wrong table, and nothing complains.

00:01:44.422 --> 00:01:46.542
So parse becomes a part of its own.

00:01:46.822 --> 00:01:51.042
It finds a table by what the table says it is, and hands on rows

00:01:51.062 --> 00:01:52.702
in one fixed shape:

00:01:52.982 --> 00:01:56.462
date, opponent, result.

00:01:59.532 --> 00:02:00.932
Now put the rows together.

00:02:01.292 --> 00:02:05.572
A match between two schools is listed on both schools' pages.

00:02:06.012 --> 00:02:09.972
Add up every row, and every match counts twice.

00:02:11.668 --> 00:02:14.428
So build is where rows become matches.

00:02:14.788 --> 00:02:18.688
It decides which copy of each match to keep, and only then works

00:02:18.708 --> 00:02:19.788
out the standings.

00:02:21.594 --> 00:02:24.354
Last, the run ends and hands you a table.

00:02:24.714 --> 00:02:26.114
Did every site answer?

00:02:26.394 --> 00:02:27.954
How many rows came in?

00:02:28.154 --> 00:02:29.394
Were there errors?

00:02:29.674 --> 00:02:31.314
The table can't say.

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:35.260
So report writes down what the run did:

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:39.900
the pages it fetched, the rows it found, and the errors it hit.

00:02:41.864 --> 00:02:44.144
Line the parts up, and you have a pipeline.

00:02:44.584 --> 00:02:48.384
Each stage takes what the last one made and hands on something new.

00:02:48.744 --> 00:02:52.864
Each can be checked on its own, and run again without starting over.

00:02:55.844 --> 00:02:57.884
Now look at what that report counts.

00:02:58.164 --> 00:03:00.764
Every line in it is about what the run did.

00:03:00.964 --> 00:03:03.404
None of it says what the run should have found.

00:03:03.684 --> 00:03:05.964
That is the question this module is about.

